<section class="page-hero"><span class="eyebrow">About PocketCat</span><h1>Building a calmer way to organize work.</h1><p>PocketCat is an early-stage SaaS productivity workspace that brings tasks, notes, projects and optional Amazon Bedrock-powered AI assistance together.</p></section><section class="section about-long"><div class="section-inner">
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">01</span><h2>Why PocketCat exists</h2><p>Modern work often becomes harder than the work itself. Tasks, notes, project trackers and documents split context across separate tools, leaving people to reconstruct decisions before they can act. PocketCat brings the next action, supporting information and intended outcome into one workspace.</p><p>The product focuses on a small set of understandable building blocks rather than a noisy interface. Users can move between personal work and project context without having to adopt a rigid methodology or maintain several disconnected systems.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">02</span><h2>A focused product philosophy</h2><p>PocketCat is built around practical utility: capture work, preserve context, connect related tasks and make progress visible. Features should earn their place by reducing friction, not by adding configuration for its own sake.</p><p>The platform is early-stage, so its public messaging distinguishes implemented functionality from optional services and illustrative metrics. That distinction is important to users evaluating the product and to administrators operating it responsibly.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">03</span><h2>Tasks turn intent into action</h2><p>Tasks provide a clear place for titles, descriptions, status, priority and due dates. Familiar states such as Todo, In Progress and Completed make the next step understandable without requiring a specialized project-management process.</p><p>Where a task belongs to a larger effort, it can be associated with a project. This keeps execution details close to the outcome they support and helps users prioritize work with the relevant context in view.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">04</span><h2>Notes preserve useful context</h2><p>Notes capture decisions, research, meeting takeaways and ideas that would otherwise be scattered across messages or temporary documents. Pinning and search help users bring important information back into view when they need it.</p><p>The notes experience is intentionally lightweight. It is designed to support active work rather than claim to replace every documentation, records-management or enterprise knowledge platform.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">05</span><h2>Projects connect work to outcomes</h2><p>Projects group related tasks around a shared objective. Descriptions, status and dates provide a compact view of what the work is intended to accomplish and where it stands.</p><p>Milestones offer an additional way to identify checkpoints. The aim is to give users enough structure to plan and review progress while avoiding unnecessary layers of hierarchy for small teams and individual work.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">06</span><h2>Amazon Bedrock is optional intelligence</h2><p>PocketCat can connect its AI assistance to Amazon Bedrock for supported model workflows. Depending on the configured connection, the application can use a Bedrock-compatible API key or AWS IAM credentials to send prompts and receive model output.</p><p>AI is an optional aid, not the authority over a user’s workspace. Users should review generated content, avoid sending information they are not authorized to disclose, and understand that prompts may be processed by the configured AWS region and model.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">07</span><h2>AI credentials stay on the server</h2><p>Bedrock credentials are handled by server-side PHP code. The application stores sensitive values through its secret-storage helpers, encrypts configured secrets at rest, and displays masked status indicators instead of returning saved values to ordinary form fields.</p><p>The browser submits settings to authenticated endpoints protected by CSRF verification. AI requests are then made by the server, so the AWS secret access key and request-signing material do not need to be embedded in frontend JavaScript.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">08</span><h2>Plans and usage controls</h2><p>Plan configuration defines workspace allowances and AI usage limits. The intent is to make the relationship between a plan and its available capacity understandable to users before they rely on a feature.</p><p>Administrators should verify pricing, quota values and billing behavior before publishing a commercial offer. A visible plan or pricing page is not, by itself, evidence that payment collection or subscription fulfillment is configured.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">09</span><h2>Administration has a separate boundary</h2><p>Administrative screens provide access to site settings, user management, security configuration, email, AI integrations and operational tools. These controls are separate from the normal user workspace and require administrator authorization.</p><p>Separating these responsibilities helps reduce accidental changes and gives operators a dedicated place to manage configuration. Production deployments should still apply least privilege to both application accounts and database credentials.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">10</span><h2>Deployment and initial configuration</h2><p>The deployment operator provisions a PHP-compatible hosting environment and database, then uses the initial setup flow to validate runtime capabilities, create the schema and provision the first administrator. Optional integrations can be configured later without being required for the core workspace.</p><p>The setup flow is a deployment-time operation, not a routine end-user workflow. An installation lock prevents it from becoming an unrestricted public reconfiguration route, and operators remain responsible for hosting permissions, backups, updates and runtime operations.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">11</span><h2>Persistent data and workspace ownership</h2><p>PocketCat uses a relational database for account records and core workspace entities. Prepared statements are used throughout the main data-access paths, and records are associated with their owning users where the feature requires isolation.</p><p>Database backups, restore tests and controlled schema changes remain essential operational responsibilities. The application archive alone is not a backup of customer data or a substitute for a documented recovery procedure.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">12</span><h2>Security is layered</h2><p>The application includes password hashing, session handling, CSRF checks on state-changing forms, server-side authorization and encrypted storage for configured secrets. These controls reduce common risks but do not remove the need for secure hosting and regular maintenance.</p><p>Operators should keep PHP and database software supported, serve the site over HTTPS, restrict file and database permissions, protect backups, and rotate credentials when exposure is suspected. Security claims should be limited to controls that are deployed and tested.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">13</span><h2>Privacy and responsible processing</h2><p>PocketCat processes account information and the content users choose to save in tasks, notes and projects. Optional integrations can transmit selected content to external providers when a user invokes those features.</p><p>Users should avoid entering passwords, access tokens or sensitive personal information into general-purpose notes or AI prompts unless they have authorization and a valid reason. Administrators should select service regions and providers that fit their privacy obligations.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">14</span><h2>A clear public product surface</h2><p>The public website explains the product, features, workflow, plans, support channels and policies before a visitor creates an account. This helps prospective users understand the purpose of the workspace and the limits of optional integrations.</p><p>Public content should remain consistent with the implementation. Links, pricing claims, support addresses and policy descriptions should be reviewed whenever routes or product behavior change.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">15</span><h2>Live counters versus illustrative charts</h2><p>The public Live Workspace counters are intended to represent configured platform activity metrics, while presentation-only growth visualizations are labeled as illustrative data. Keeping these categories distinct prevents sample figures from being mistaken for audited business results.</p><p>Before using metrics in investor, customer or grant materials, confirm their source, refresh behavior and meaning. Where values are manually configured for demonstration, label them accordingly and do not describe them as independently verified production measurements.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">16</span><h2>A responsive workspace</h2><p>PocketCat’s navigation and workspace pages are designed to remain usable on smaller screens. Responsive layouts prioritize readable content, visible actions and navigation that can be reached without relying on a desktop-sized viewport.</p><p>Mobile polish is more than shrinking a desktop layout. Release checks should include forms, menus, tables, chart labels, focus states and long content at common phone widths and with browser zoom enabled.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">17</span><h2>Search and discovery</h2><p>Search provides a shared entry point for finding tasks, notes and projects. It reduces the need to remember which screen contains a particular piece of context and supports returning to previous work.</p><p>Search results must continue to respect authentication and ownership boundaries. A useful discovery feature is only reliable when it reveals the right records to the right account and handles empty or failed searches clearly.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">18</span><h2>Support and feedback</h2><p>Support questions and product feedback can be sent to support@pocketcat.online. Clear reports are most useful when they include the affected page, the expected result and the steps needed to reproduce the problem.</p><p>Do not include passwords, API keys, session cookies or other credentials in support messages. For suspected security issues, provide a concise description and enough non-sensitive detail to help the team investigate safely.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">19</span><h2>Honest limits and dependencies</h2><p>PocketCat depends on the configured hosting environment, database, mail provider and any optional external integrations. Those services can have separate availability, pricing, quotas, regional processing and policy requirements.</p><p>The product does not claim that a configured feature is production-ready solely because its settings screen exists. Deployment owners should test the real provider connection, operational monitoring, backup recovery and user journey before making availability or compliance commitments.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">20</span><h2>A practical path forward</h2><p>PocketCat’s direction is to make everyday work easier to capture, understand and complete while keeping administration and optional AI capabilities understandable. Improvements should be guided by real user needs, reliability findings and evidence from testing.</p><p>The long-term objective is a workspace that earns trust through clear behavior, responsible handling of data and straightforward operations. That means documenting limitations, fixing defects promptly and validating important changes before release.</p></article>
